  "summary": "The architecture of Uruguay is influenced by the country's gentle geography, its relatively recent history, and its melting-pot culture, bearing a strong European imprint.",
  "description": "The architecture of Uruguay is influenced by the country's gentle geography, its relatively recent history, and its melting-pot culture, bearing a strong European imprint.",
  "culturalContext": "No significant architectural remains was left of the ancient native cultures. Beginning with the Spanish colonisation could be seen the very beginnings of local construction; but the establishment of populated places took a long time compared with Argentina and other neighboring regions. Santo Domingo Soriano, the oldest locality, was established as a mission in 1624; Colonia del Sacramento, a Portuguese outpost, in 1680; and the future capital city, Montevideo, in 1726, taking advantage of its namesake bay, a natural harbour. Notable examples of early architecture in Uruguay are the ruins of Saint Francis Xavier Convent in Colonia, San Miguel Fort, Santa Teresa Fortress, Saint Charles Church, the Cathedral and Cabildo of Montevideo.",
